# libcss: :is() and :where() match (forgiving selector lists)
#
# MacSurf's fixes359 parsed :is()/:where()/:has() by swallowing the
# arguments; the matcher never matched them. Tailwind v4 writes a lot of
# CSS with them -- claude.ai defines its whole colour palette under
# ':where(:root,:host)' -- so none of it applied (button fills, borders,
# surfaces all resolved var(--cds-*) to nothing).
#
# - parse: the argument is split at top-level commas and each alternative
#   parsed with parseSelector on its own; alternatives that fail (':host',
#   pseudo-elements, anything this libcss lacks) are dropped, per the
#   forgiving-list rule. Stored in a new detail value type, SELLIST
#   (value_type widened to 2 bits), owned by the selector.
# - specificity: :where() adds 0, :is() the max of its arguments.
# - select: match_chain_at() matches one argument as a complex selector
#   with the node as subject (checks the subject's element name itself,
#   no reject cache, no cascade); any argument matching matches.
# - :has() stays V1 (never matches), but its argument skip now counts
#   FUNCTION tokens, so ':has(:not(.x))' no longer ends the pseudo early
#   and invalidates the whole rule.
# - :is()/:where() inside :not() are still rejected at parse (fixes360b).
